good.

> 1. Are there any monsters that will loot my stash if I don't lock it?

No. But they (especially gelcubes and nymphs) may just run away with the 
box.

> Any advantage to using a chest vs. a large box?

Not that I know of. You may even use a bag.

> 2. Prayer timeout: I've seen lots of references that there is one, but
> how do you calculate the actual value?

Somewhere along the lines of (350 turns plus 1000 turnd if crowned) 
times a factor between 3/XL and XL/3 (or between 1/5 and 5 if your level 
is greater than 15).

> 3. Tossing food at monsters (ponies/horses/etc.) can make them "peaceful"
> but is it possible to make them "tame" on food alone, or do you need
> charm monster/taming magic?

yes. Use food they'll eat (apples, carrots, lichen corpses...)

> 4. Rusted/burnt and corroded/rotted: Are these "erosion" damages somehow
> paired the way I've listed them here? 

you get burn/rot for leather, wood and cloth and rust/corrode for 
metals. IIRC !oacid corrode metals and rot organics while !oil rust/burn.

> I know about reading enchant
> armor/weapon while confused, and that !oOil restores rusted and corroded
> items... 

only weapons.

> any other way to restore burnt and rotted items?

enchant armour/weapon while confused. If the item in question is not a 
weapon/armour, you can't.

> 5. I've seen references to naming one's weapons Sting or Orcrist; what
> does that do? (I use daggers.)

It turns your elven dagger (elven broadsword) into the artifact weapon 
Sting (Orcrist) with +d5 to-hit, double damage vs. orcs and (in case of 
Sting) warning against orcs. Since orcs are quite harmless this isn't of 
much use except for low-level elven wizards who have trouble hitting 
things with daggers and to get them out of the way so your god will drop 
you Stormy as a gift.

> 6. I know some of the dangers of turn-to-stone corpses. *trice corpses
> (even blessed ones) rot away over time; any way to preserve them?

put them into an icebox, store them on ice, revive them.

